| 2 | 0 | boundary; no comparison | false | 1 |
| 3 | 1 | 1@1 : 3@0 | false | 2 |
| 4 | 2 | 3@0 : 2@2 | true | 1 |
| 5 | 1 | 1@1 : 2@2 | false | 2 |
| 6 | 2 | 2@2 : 3@0 | false | 3 |

Output [1, 2, 3]; original indices [1, 2, 0]; visits 7; key comparisons 5; swaps 2; complete records 9.
\image(figures/gnome_pairs.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Only the two actual swapping visits are drawn; the table includes every visit. Original identities travel with their values. The three-value sort has seven visits and nine complete records because initial and stop metadata also count.)
\alttext(Two rows show key three at original position zero exchanging first with one at original position one, then with two at original position two. Each arrow leads to the actual exchanged pair and decremented pointer. A table prints all seven visits.)

> Why the walk stops, and why it is stable

Before each visit, the prefix a[0..i) is sorted. Advancing at the boundary creates
a sorted one-element prefix. Advancing over an ordered pair extends an already
sorted prefix. After a swap and backward step, the shorter prefix excludes the
changed positions and remains sorted. When i=n, the whole sequence is sorted.

Let I be the current number of strict inversions: pairs of positions p<q with
a[p]>a[q]. Exchanging a strict adjacent inversion removes exactly one inversion;
relations with every other value have the same total contribution. Equal values
are never exchanged, so equal-key identities cannot cross.

The pointer can retreat, but the following integer potential cannot increase:

$$
P=n-i+2I.
$$
\alttext(The potential is the remaining pointer distance n minus i, plus twice the current number of strict inversions.)

An ordered forward visit increases i by one without changing I, decreasing P by
one. A swapping backward visit decreases i by one and I by one, also decreasing
P by one. P starts at n+2I0, remains nonnegative, and is zero after sorting stops.
Thus every visit consumes exactly one unit of the initial potential:

$$
S=n+2I_0,\qquad B=I_0,\qquad F=n+I_0,\qquad C=S-Z.
$$
\alttext(Total loop visits S equal n plus twice the input inversions. Backward steps B equal inversions, forward steps F equal n plus inversions, and key comparisons C equal visits minus boundary visits Z.)

Here Z counts visits at index zero. For nonempty input it is one plus the number
of strict new prefix minima in the original sequence: each such value crosses
the entire earlier prefix and causes one extra return to zero. Equal minima do
not cross. For empty input every work count is zero.

The retained boundary/ordered-pair rule is documented by
[Paul E. Black’s NIST entry](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/gnomeSort.html), which
also records Hamid Sarbazi-Azad’s 2000 *Stupid Sort* newsletter article. The
potential and the controlled counts here make the vague phrase “nearly sorted”
precise: time is linear when I0=O(n). Repeated forward checks mean this loop need
not perform the same number of comparisons as an ordinary insertion sort.

> Retain the original seven-value walk

The original input [5,3,8,1,9,2,7] has ten strict inversions. The predicted visit
count is therefore 7+2×10=27. Initial and stopping metadata are not loop visits;
the pointer plot uses only the actual step records, including the initial
boundary visit at zero and the last visit before reaching the end.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/gnome_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/gnome_sort.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

 const std::vector<int> input{5,3,8,1,9,2,7};
 const auto t=dm::algorithms::gnome_sort_trace(input);
 std::vector<double> visit,pointer;
 std::print("| visit | pointer before | pointer after | key comparison | swap |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | --- |\n");
 for(const auto& e:t.events)if(e.kind==dm::algorithms::GnomeSortEventKind::step){
  visit.push_back(double(e.ordinal));pointer.push_back(double(e.index_before));
  std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",e.ordinal,e.index_before,e.index_after,e.compared,e.swapped);
 }
 std::print("\nSorted {}; visits {}; forward {}; backward {}; comparisons {}; boundary visits {}; retained records {}; caller input unchanged {}.\n",t.run.output,t.run.work.visits,t.run.work.forward_steps,t.run.work.backward_steps,t.run.work.key_comparisons,t.run.work.boundary_visits,t.events.size(),input==std::vector<int>{5,3,8,1,9,2,7});
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
 p.line(visit,pointer,"pointer",{37,99,235},2).points(visit,pointer,"pointer",{37,99,235},4)
  .legend({"Before-visit pointer"},{{37,99,235}})
  .title("Original seven-value gnome walk").xlab("Actual loop visit").ylab("Pointer before visit")
  .x_limits(0,26).y_limits(0,7).save_svg("build/gnome_original.svg");
 if(t.run.work.visits!=27||t.run.work.swaps!=10||t.run.work.key_comparisons!=24)throw std::runtime_error("original walk");

}
