// result_end
\caption(The two-value sort takes four visits and six complete records; five records are insufficient. Empty input still needs initial and stop metadata. A four-value-per-array family occupies sixteen integer entries, admitted as one whole generation.)
\alttext(Two bar charts compare six complete history records with rejected cap five, and sixteen generated integer entries with rejected cap fifteen. Printed controls also check empty metadata, optional quotas disabled by zero, independent copies and unchanged caller input.)

> What the experiments establish

A sorted prefix gives correctness, strict adjacent exchanges give stability, and
the potential counts every forward and backward visit. The original example,
identity-labelled duplicates, controlled inversion families and boundary counts
agree with those derivations. Independent development tests use direct stable
ranks and pairwise inversion counts; actual recorded transitions also certify the
prefix invariant and one-unit potential decrease at every visit.

There is no external dataset in this experiment. Controlled integer inputs give
known inversion counts and exact predictions, including empty and repeated values.
The ordinary sort keeps constant auxiliary state beyond its owned input/output,
while diagnostic identities and retained history have their separate stated
storage costs. A short loop is easy to describe; its visit count, key comparisons
and retained records are still different quantities.
